First Author: Gholamzadeh
Title: Analysis of the most influential factors affecting outcomes of lung transplant recipients: a multivariate prediction model based on UNOS Data
Source number: 14  ￼

Very short summary: This study developed machine-learning regression models to predict lung-transplant waiting-list priority scores using UNOS data. Five key findings were: (1) after preprocessing, the final analytic dataset contained 32,966 records and 15 features; (2) six regression models were compared, and the random forest regressor performed best; (3) the target outcome was the continuous lung-transplant priority/allocation score rather than post-transplant survival; (4) SHAP identified 11 key predictors, including oxygen requirement, diagnosis group, waiting time, medical condition, hemodynamics, ventilator need, support variables, cigarette use, and transfusion events; and (5) the authors deployed the final model as a web-based Streamlit decision-support tool. The paper explicitly notes that the model still requires external validation in other populations and healthcare systems.

